% Curated coach notes migrated from src/content/annotations.ts (v1).
% Keyed by position + move when parsed, so transpositions share them.

[Event "Curated note 1"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 { The king's pawn advance — Fischer called it 'best by test'. White grabs central space and opens lines for the queen and king's bishop. } *

[Event "Curated note 2"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 { The queen's pawn opening. White occupies the center with a pawn that is already defended, steering toward rich positional play. } *

[Event "Curated note 3"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. c4 { The English Opening. White fights for the central d5-square from the flank, keeping maximum flexibility over the pawn structure. } *

[Event "Curated note 4"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. Nf3 { The Réti move order: White develops first and postpones the central decision, often transposing into d4 or c4 systems. } *

[Event "Curated note 5"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 { Black mirrors, claiming an equal share of the center. The classical open games begin. } *

[Event "Curated note 6"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 { Develops with tempo by attacking the e5-pawn — the most natural move in chess. } *

[Event "Curated note 7"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Defends e5 while developing. Almost universal here. } *

[Event "Curated note 8"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 { The Ruy Lopez. The bishop pressures the knight that defends e5, beginning a long-term siege of Black's center. One of the oldest and deepest openings in chess. } *

[Event "Curated note 9"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 { The Morphy Defense. Black puts the question to the bishop immediately: retreat or trade? } *

[Event "Curated note 10"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 { Keeps the tension. After a later ...b5 the bishop drops back to b3, still eyeing f7. } *

[Event "Curated note 11"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Develops and counterattacks e4 — the mainline of the mainline. } *

[Event "Curated note 12"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O { White ignores the threat to e4: after ...Nxe4 the open Ruy gives White strong central play with d4 and Re1. } *

[Event "Curated note 13"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 { The Italian Game. The bishop takes aim at f7, the weakest square in Black's camp. } *

[Event "Curated note 14"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 { The Giuoco Piano — 'quiet game'. Both sides develop classically; the battle revolves around the d4-break. } *

[Event "Curated note 15"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 { Prepares d4, building a big center. The modern main line keeps the tension with a slow build-up. } *

[Event "Curated note 16"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 { The Two Knights Defense. Black develops actively and dares White to grab material with Ng5. } *

[Event "Curated note 17"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 { The Scotch Game. White breaks the central tension at once, trading the d-pawn for free development and open lines. } *

[Event "Curated note 18"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 { White recentralizes the knight. The half-open position gives both sides easy piece play. } *

[Event "Curated note 19"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 { The Vienna Game. White defends e4 first and keeps the f2-f4 advance in reserve — a King's Gambit with insurance. } *

[Event "Curated note 20"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e5 2. f4 { The King's Gambit! White offers a pawn to rip open the f-file and seize the center — the romantic era distilled into one move. } *

[Event "Curated note 21"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 { The Sicilian Defense — the most combative reply to 1.e4. Black fights for the center asymmetrically, trading a wing pawn for a central one and winning the long-term pawn-structure battle. } *

[Event "Curated note 22"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 { The main road to the Open Sicilian. White prepares d4. } *

[Event "Curated note 23"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 { Black restrains e5 and opens the c8-bishop's diagonal, keeping the choice between Najdorf, Dragon and Scheveningen setups. } *

[Event "Curated note 24"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 { White strikes in the center — the Open Sicilian promises sharp play and opposite-side attacking races. } *

[Event "Curated note 25"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Develops with tempo against e4, forcing Nc3 before White can set up the ideal c4 bind. } *

[Event "Curated note 26"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 { The Najdorf Variation — the Rolls-Royce of the Sicilian. The little pawn move controls b5, prepares ...e5 and ...b5, and keeps every plan available. Kasparov's and Fischer's life-long weapon. } *

[Event "Curated note 27"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 { The Dragon Variation. The bishop will breathe fire on g7; White usually castles long and both sides attack with everything. } *

[Event "Curated note 28"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { A flexible developing move heading for Sveshnikov, Taimanov or classical setups. } *

[Event "Curated note 29"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 { The Taimanov/Kan complex: Black keeps the d-pawn flexible and prepares quick queenside play. } *

[Event "Curated note 30"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. c3 { The Alapin. White sidesteps the Open Sicilian and simply builds the d4-center — a favorite practical weapon. } *

[Event "Curated note 31"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 { The Closed Sicilian move order: White delays or avoids d4 and plans a kingside build-up with g3 and f4. } *

[Event "Curated note 32"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e6 { The French Defense. Black lets White build the center only to attack it with ...d5 next move — solid, strategic, and famously resilient. } *

[Event "Curated note 33"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 { White takes the full center on offer. } *

[Event "Curated note 34"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 { The thematic counterstrike. The central tension defines the whole opening: advance, exchange, or defend e4? } *

[Event "Curated note 35"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { The Advance Variation. White gains space and a kingside attack skeleton; Black will chip at the pawn chain with ...c5 and ...f6. } *

[Event "Curated note 36"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 { The main line, defending e4 and inviting the sharp Winawer with ...Bb4. } *

[Event "Curated note 37"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 { The Winawer. Black pins, doubles White's pawns after ...Bxc3, and starts a fight of structure versus dynamism. } *

[Event "Curated note 38"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 { The Tarrasch Variation: like Nc3 but without allowing the pin — at the cost of blocking the c1-bishop for a while. } *

[Event "Curated note 39"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c6 { The Caro-Kann Defense. Black prepares ...d5 with the pawn, keeping the light-squared bishop's diagonal open — the French without the bad bishop. } *

[Event "Curated note 40"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 { Black challenges the center immediately, fully developed on principle. } *

[Event "Curated note 41"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { The Advance Caro. White grabs space; Black's 'good' bishop comes out to f5 before ...e6. } *

[Event "Curated note 42"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 { The point of the Caro-Kann: the bishop escapes before the pawn chain locks it in. } *

[Event "Curated note 43"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 { The Classical Caro-Kann. Black develops the bishop actively and builds an ultra-solid structure White must work hard to dent. } *

[Event "Curated note 44"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 d5 { The Scandinavian Defense: the most direct challenge to e4 in chess — Black forces matters on move one. } *

[Event "Curated note 45"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 { The queen recaptures early and will be kicked by Nc3, but Black argues the lost tempo buys a rock-solid structure. } *

[Event "Curated note 46"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qa5 { The classical retreat, keeping the queen active on the a5–e1 diagonal. } *

[Event "Curated note 47"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 d6 { The Pirc/Modern complex: Black concedes the center temporarily, planning to strike it with pieces and pawn breaks once White overextends. } *

[Event "Curated note 48"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 Nf6 { Alekhine's Defense — provocation as strategy. The knight invites White's pawns forward so Black can attack the overextended chain. } *

[Event "Curated note 49"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. e4 g6 { The Modern Defense. Black fianchettoes first and decides on a central setup later. } *

[Event "Curated note 50"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 d5 { Black stakes a symmetrical claim in the center. } *

[Event "Curated note 51"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 { The Queen's Gambit. Not a real gambit — White offers the c-pawn to deflect Black's d-pawn and rule the center; the pawn is always regainable. } *

[Event "Curated note 52"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 { The Queen's Gambit Declined. Black keeps the strong point on d5 at the cost of the c8-bishop's freedom — the classical battleground of world championship chess. } *

[Event "Curated note 53"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 { The Slav Defense. Black supports d5 while keeping the c8-bishop's diagonal open — the structurally purest answer to the Queen's Gambit. } *

[Event "Curated note 54"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 { The Queen's Gambit Accepted. Black takes the pawn and will return it for rapid development and ...c5 counterplay. } *

[Event "Curated note 55"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 { The pin on the f6-knight increases pressure on d5 — the classical main line of the QGD. } *

[Event "Curated note 56"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 { The main-line Slav: Black grabs the pawn at the right moment, when ...b5 can defend it. } *

[Event "Curated note 57"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 { The Indian move order: Black develops first and only then decides how to challenge the center. } *

[Event "Curated note 58"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 { Heading for the Nimzo-Indian or Queen's Indian, the most respected of all defenses to 1.d4. } *

[Event "Curated note 59"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 { The Nimzo-Indian Defense. The pin fights for e4 without a pawn: Black may double White's c-pawns and blockade them forever. } *

[Event "Curated note 60"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 { The Queen's Indian. The bishop on b7 joins the fight for e4; a strategic cousin of the Nimzo. } *

[Event "Curated note 61"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 { Black prepares the king's fianchetto — King's Indian or Grünfeld territory. } *

[Event "Curated note 62"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 { The King's Indian Defense. Black concedes the center now to bomb it later with ...e5 or ...c5 — the ultimate counterattacking system. } *

[Event "Curated note 63"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 { The Grünfeld Defense. Black strikes at the center immediately, betting that White's imposing pawn wall will become a target. } *

[Event "Curated note 64"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 f5 { The Dutch Defense. Black fights for e4 with the f-pawn — ambitious and double-edged, loosening the king to gain attacking chances. } *

[Event "Curated note 65"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 { The Benoni move order — Black provokes d5 and will undermine it with ...e6 or ...b5. } *

[Event "Curated note 66"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 b5 { The Benko Gambit: a full pawn for enduring queenside pressure that lasts deep into the endgame. } *

[Event "Curated note 67"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 { The London System. White develops the bishop outside the pawn chain before playing e3, building the same solid setup against nearly anything. } *

[Event "Curated note 68"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 { The modern London move order, keeping options against the Indian setups. } *

[Event "Curated note 69"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 { The classical London build: c3, e3, Bd3, Nbd2 and O-O come almost regardless of Black's setup. } *

[Event "Curated note 70"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. c4 e5 { The reversed Sicilian: Black plays a Sicilian a tempo down, White a Sicilian a tempo up. } *

[Event "Curated note 71"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. c4 c5 { The Symmetrical English — a subtle fight where the first to break symmetry takes the risk and the chances. } *

[Event "Curated note 72"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. c4 Nf6 { Flexible development; Black waits to see White's central plan. } *

[Event "Curated note 73"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. c4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 { The Four Knights English, the sound backbone of the 1.c4 e5 complex. } *

[Event "Curated note 74"]
[Annotator "chess-openings-trainer v1"]

1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 { The King's Indian Attack setup: White fianchettoes and plays the same healthy structure against anything Black chooses. } *
